---
<Layout
  fullTitle="Columbus Issue 8 & Issue 9 (2026): Ballot Language, Explained"
  title="Issues 8 and 9: how Columbus elects its City Council is on the ballot"
  description="A nonpartisan guide to Columbus Issues 8 and 9 on the Nov. 3, 2026 ballot: the certified ballot language, what each charter amendment does, and what happens if both pass."
  ribbon={false}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[articleLd, faqLd]}
>
  <div class="wrap"><div class="article">
    <section class="art-hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">Columbus Issues 8 &amp; 9 &middot; General Election, November 3, 2026</div>
      <h1>Issues 8 and 9: how Columbus elects its City Council is on the ballot</h1>
      <p class="dek">This November, Columbus voters will decide <strong>two proposed charter amendments</strong> on how City Council is elected. <strong>Issue&nbsp;8</strong>, placed on the ballot by citizen petition, would have each council member elected only by the voters in their own district. <strong>Issue&nbsp;9</strong>, referred by Council, makes the same change and also adds four members elected citywide, for a 13-member council. Council members and the petition&rsquo;s organizers describe the two as <strong>complementary rather than competing</strong>.</p>
      <div class="asof"><span class="dot"></span> Updated {updated} &middot; ballot language certified by the Franklin County Board of Elections</div>
      <div class="keyfacts">
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">What&rsquo;s on the ballot</div><div class="kfv">Issue 8 and Issue 9</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">How they qualified</div><div class="kfv">Issue 8 by citizen petition; Issue 9 referred by Council</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">When you vote</div><div class="kfv">Tue, Nov.&nbsp;3, 2026 &middot; early voting from Oct.&nbsp;6</div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="callout"><b>What&rsquo;s new.</b> The Franklin County Board of Elections has certified both measures: the citizen petition is <strong>Issue&nbsp;8</strong> and Council&rsquo;s amendment is <strong>Issue&nbsp;9</strong>. The Board&rsquo;s official ballot language is quoted below, with links to the full text of each amendment. On Sept.&nbsp;22, the Franklin County Democratic Party endorsed both issues (<a href={u("wosu0923", "whats_new")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>).</div>

    <h2>The system today</h2>
    <p>Columbus uses a <strong>hybrid</strong> system, in place since the 2023 elections. There are nine council members, and each must <strong>live in the district they represent</strong> &mdash; but every voter in the city votes in <strong>all nine</strong> district races. So members represent a district geographically while answering to the whole city. Both issues would end citywide voting for district seats; Issue&nbsp;9 would also add seats elected citywide.</p>

    <h2>The ballot language</h2>
    <p>This is the wording you&rsquo;ll see on your ballot, as published in the Board of Elections&rsquo; official notices of election. Each issue is a separate yes-or-no question.</p>

    <div class="ballot-lang">
      <div class="bh"><span class="iss">Issue 8</span><span class="who">Proposed charter amendment &middot; by petition</span></div>
      <blockquote>
        {ballot8.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
        <p class="q">{QUESTION}</p>
      </blockquote>
      <div class="docs">
        <a href={u("notice8", "issue8_notice")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official notice of election (PDF) &rarr;</a>
        <a href={u("full8", "issue8_full_text")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full text of the amendment (PDF) &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ballot-lang">
      <div class="bh"><span class="iss">Issue 9</span><span class="who">Proposed charter amendment &middot; by Council</span></div>
      <blockquote>
        {ballot9Lead.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
      </blockquote>
      <details>
        <summary>Show the rest of the ballot language ({ballot9Rest.length} more paragraphs)</summary>
        <blockquote>
          {ballot9Rest.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
        </blockquote>
      </details>
      <blockquote class="qwrap"><p class="q">{QUESTION}</p></blockquote>
      <div class="docs">
        <a href={u("notice9", "issue9_notice")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official notice of election (PDF) &rarr;</a>
        <a href={u("full9", "issue9_full_text")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Full text of the amendment (PDF) &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>The two issues, side by side</h2>
    <div class="compare2">
      <div class="sys prop">
        <div class="sh"><div class="lab">Issue 8 &middot; Citizen petition (Our City, Our Say)</div><div class="nm">District-only elections</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">What it changes</div><div class="sa">Switches council to district-only elections: only voters who live in a district elect that district&rsquo;s member. Keeps nine seats and the current map.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">How you&rsquo;d vote</div><div class="sa">One council race on your ballot &mdash; your district&rsquo;s &mdash; alongside citywide races such as mayor.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Council size</div><div class="sa">Stays at nine members.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">At-large seats</div><div class="sa">None.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">District map</div><div class="sa">Keeps the current map. Under the existing charter, the next redistricting commission is appointed in 2031.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Recall</div><div class="sa">Only a district&rsquo;s own voters could recall its member. A petition would need signatures equal to 15% of the district&rsquo;s turnout in the last mayoral election.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Candidate petitions</div><div class="sa">Unchanged: 250 signatures for district candidates.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Council rules</div><div class="sa">Unchanged.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Status</div><div class="sa">Certified as Issue&nbsp;8. It qualified after the Board of Elections validated 15,945 petition signatures. The Board&rsquo;s notice says Council passed the ordinance submitting it on Aug.&nbsp;31, 2026.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sys today">
        <div class="sh"><div class="lab">Issue 9 &middot; Referred by City Council</div><div class="nm">District-only elections plus four at-large seats</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">What it changes</div><div class="sa">Makes the same switch to district-only elections for the nine district seats, adds four seats elected citywide, redraws the district map, and updates council rules.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">How you&rsquo;d vote</div><div class="sa">Your district&rsquo;s race, plus the at-large races citywide once they begin in 2027.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Council size</div><div class="sa">Grows from nine to 11 in January 2028, then to 13 in January 2030.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">At-large seats</div><div class="sa">Four, with four-year terms: two elected in 2027 (seated 2028) and two elected in 2029 (seated 2030).</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">District map</div><div class="sa">Redrawn in 2027. A citizen-led districting commission, appointed by March&nbsp;1, 2027, has nine months to submit three plans, and Council must adopt one by the end of that year. The new map is first used in the 2029 elections. The allowed population gap between districts rises from 1% to 7.5%.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Recall</div><div class="sa">The same district-only recall as Issue&nbsp;8. At-large members could be recalled by voters citywide.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Candidate petitions</div><div class="sa">District candidates need 150 signatures (down from 250) and one year of residency in the district before the primary. At-large candidates need 250 signatures citywide.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Council rules</div><div class="sa">Expelling a member takes a two-thirds vote. The quorum and the votes needed to pass legislation rise as council grows.</div></div>
        <div class="srow"><div class="sq">Status</div><div class="sa">Certified as Issue&nbsp;9. Council passed the ordinance placing it on the ballot on Aug.&nbsp;24, 2026; it was introduced by Council President Pro Tempore Rob Dorans.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="fine">According to the <a href={u("dispatch0824", "compare_note")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Columbus Dispatch</a>, Council voted 8&ndash;0 on Aug.&nbsp;24 to send each amendment to voters; Councilmember Melissa Green was absent but had indicated support for both. Dorans said he worked with the City Attorney&rsquo;s office so that Council&rsquo;s amendment would not conflict with the citizen petition. The redraw timeline follows the <a href={u("full9", "compare_note")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">certified text</a>; some earlier news coverage described the redraw as happening in 2028.</p>

    <h2>What happens if one or both pass?</h2>
    <div class="outcomes">
      <div class="oc"><div class="ok">Both fail</div><div class="ov">No change</div><p>Today&rsquo;s hybrid system stays: nine district seats, with every voter voting in all nine races.</p></div>
      <div class="oc"><div class="ok">Only Issue 8 passes</div><div class="ov">Nine members, district-only</div><p>Each member is elected only by the voters in their district, starting with the 2027 council elections. The current map stays.</p></div>
      <div class="oc"><div class="ok">Only Issue 9 passes</div><div class="ov">Nine district seats plus four at-large</div><p>District seats become district-only, and four citywide seats are phased in: two elected in 2027 and two in 2029. A new map is drawn in 2027 for the 2029 elections.</p></div>
      <div class="oc"><div class="ok">Both pass</div><div class="ov">A 13-member council</div><p>Both make the same change to how district seats are elected, using identical charter wording, and Issue&nbsp;9 adds the at-large seats and other changes on top. <a href={u("wosu0923", "both_pass")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU reports</a> the result would be nine members elected only by their districts plus four elected citywide.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Can I vote yes on both?</h2>
    <p>Yes. Issue&nbsp;8 and Issue&nbsp;9 are separate questions on your ballot, and you vote on each one independently &mdash; you can support one, both, or neither. Council President Shannon Hardin and several members have said they will encourage voters to approve both, and on Sept.&nbsp;22 the Franklin County Democratic Party endorsed both (<a href={u("wosu0923", "vote_both")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>). A spokesperson for Our City, Our Say has said the group won&rsquo;t spend its energy opposing Issue&nbsp;9, because it doesn&rsquo;t contradict their amendment (<a href={u("wosu0821", "vote_both")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU, Aug.&nbsp;21</a>).</p>

    <h2>How we got here</h2>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2018</div><div class="ty">Voters approve districts</div><div class="td">Columbus ends its century-old fully at-large council, adopting the hybrid district system and expanding council from seven seats to nine.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2023</div><div class="ty">First district elections</div><div class="td">All nine district seats are filled for the first time under the new system.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2025</div><div class="ty">A split result in District 7</div><div class="td">Districts 1, 3, 4, and 7 are up. In District 7, Tiara Ross wins the seat by 1,518 votes citywide, while challenger Jesse Vogel carries 61.8% in precincts mostly within the district, according to an <a href={u("abc60924", "timeline_2025")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">ABC6 analysis</a>. Our City, Our Say began pushing for reform after that race (<a href={u("wosu0923", "timeline_2025")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>).</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">Summer 2026</div><div class="ty">A petition qualifies</div><div class="td">Our City, Our Say submits about <a href={u("wosu0716", "timeline_petition")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">23,000 signatures</a> for district-only elections; the Board of Elections validates 15,945 &mdash; enough to reach the ballot.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">Aug. 2026</div><div class="ty">No single compromise &mdash; but a cooperative turn</div><div class="td">The two sides don&rsquo;t reach a formal deal. Council President Pro Tem Rob Dorans develops a second amendment, a 13-member council, that he says is meant to complement the citizen measure, not oppose it (<a href={u("wosu0821", "timeline_aug")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>).</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">Aug. 24, 2026</div><div class="ty">Council sends both to voters</div><div class="td">At its first meeting back from summer recess, City Council votes to send both amendments to the November ballot. The <a href={u("dispatch0824", "timeline_aug24")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Columbus Dispatch</a> reports each passed 8&ndash;0.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">Aug. 31, 2026</div><div class="ty">Petition ordinance passes</div><div class="td">Council passes the ordinance formally submitting the citizen petition, according to the Board of Elections&rsquo; <a href={u("notice8", "timeline_aug31")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">official notice</a>.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">Sept. 2026</div><div class="ty">Certified as Issues 8 and 9</div><div class="td">The Board of Elections certifies the <a href={u("cqi", "timeline_certified")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">November ballot</a> and assigns issue numbers. Its official notices of election, with the ballot language, are dated Sept.&nbsp;14.</div></div>
      <div class="tl now"><div class="tdate">Sept. 22, 2026</div><div class="ty">County Democrats endorse both</div><div class="td">The Franklin County Democratic Party&rsquo;s central committee votes to endorse Issues 8 and 9 (<a href={u("wosu0923", "timeline_sept22")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>).</div></div>
      <div class="tl future"><div class="tdate">Oct. 5&ndash;6, 2026</div><div class="ty">Registration closes; early voting opens</div><div class="td">Register or update your registration by Oct.&nbsp;5. Early in-person voting begins Oct.&nbsp;6.</div></div>
      <div class="tl future"><div class="tdate">November 3, 2026</div><div class="ty">Columbus votes</div><div class="td">Voters decide Issue&nbsp;8 and Issue&nbsp;9.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h2>What each side says</h2>
    <div class="procon">
      <div class="pc for">
        <h3>Arguments made for the changes</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Supporters of Our City, Our Say say district-only elections give neighborhoods a representative who answers to them directly, and that running in one district costs less than a citywide campaign. &ldquo;Every resident will have their own city council member elected by the residents in each district directly accountable to them and to the communities within each district,&rdquo; organizer Will Petrik said (<a href={u("wosu0923", "procon")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>).</li>
          <li>Jesse Vogel, who carried District 7&rsquo;s precincts in 2025 but lost citywide, said of that result: &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t make sense to voters. It sows distrust and makes accountability hard&rdquo; (<a href={u("abc60924", "procon")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">ABC6</a>).</li>
          <li>Dorans says Issue&nbsp;9&rsquo;s four at-large seats keep a citywide voice on issues that cross district lines, such as transit, housing, and workforce development (<a href={u("wosu0821", "procon")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>). &ldquo;These two issues work in tandem together to complement one another,&rdquo; he told county Democrats (<a href={u("wosu0923", "procon_dorans")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>).</li>
          <li>&ldquo;As our city grows, we need to give more folks a seat at the table and make sure that every neighborhood has a champion,&rdquo; Council President Shannon Hardin said (<a href={u("abc60924", "procon_hardin")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">ABC6</a>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="pc against">
        <h3>Concerns and open questions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Some labor and Black community leaders have raised concerns about how the changes could affect representation on council (<a href={u("wosu0818", "procon")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU, Aug.&nbsp;18</a>). Issue&nbsp;9 does not require majority-minority districts, which some Black leaders had called for (<a href={u("wosu0821", "procon_mm")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU, Aug.&nbsp;21</a>).</li>
          <li>Some Our City, Our Say supporters, including former mayoral candidate Joe Motil, back Issue&nbsp;8 but criticize Issue&nbsp;9 (<a href={u("wosu0923", "procon_motil")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU</a>). A coalition spokesperson said four at-large seats weren&rsquo;t preferable to many members, though the group isn&rsquo;t opposing Issue&nbsp;9 (<a href={u("wosu0821", "procon_ocos")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">WOSU, Aug.&nbsp;21</a>).</li>
          <li>Council chief of staff John Tannous, arguing for citywide seats, said research shows that &ldquo;when cities move to a district-based council, they tend to see housing production drop dramatically.&rdquo; District 7 Councilmember Tiara Ross said a citywide perspective is needed on housing (<a href={u("abc60924", "procon_citywide")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">ABC6</a>).</li>
          <li>With four new seats, a 2027 map redraw, and new council rules, Issue&nbsp;9 is a larger structural change than Issue&nbsp;8.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="fine">Our City, Our Say organizers, including Maitland Neff and Jesse Vogel, have said they do not view Council&rsquo;s measure as contradictory and regard the effort as good-faith. <strong>Endorsements reported so far:</strong> Franklin County Democratic Party &mdash; Issue&nbsp;8 and Issue&nbsp;9 (Sept.&nbsp;22, 2026). The points above summarize positions publicly stated by the named parties; they are not endorsements. Columbus Districts is a nonpartisan reference and does not take a position on either issue.</p>

